-- Agent-policy extraction prompts v2 (stage 2, task 9b): fixes from the real-model acceptance run.
--  1. The comparison wording table: "above" had become gte.
--  2. deciders / notify / owner roles: the approver had landed in owner.
-- Neither prompt offers an example policy or a concrete value to copy.
-- Updates the two rows by prompt_name; apply to BOTH bp_testdb and bp_sqldb. Idempotent.
-- Rollback: 2026-10-10_agent_policy_prompts_wording_rollback.sql

UPDATE proc.bp_prompt SET prompts_desc = $p${"prompt_template": "You read one part of a company policy document and turn each rule it states into a proposed policy for AI agents. A person checks every proposal before it can take effect; nothing you return is confirmed or Active.\n\nDocument: {document_title}, version {document_version}.\n\nBusiness areas and their sub-areas. These are the only ones that exist:\n{taxonomy}\n\nThe registry: what the orchestrator can see. First every checkpoint, then, for each checkpoint that is checked today, the actions (tools) it sees and the inputs it receives there, as field (type, where it comes from):\n{registry}\n\nRead every section below in one pass. Return every policy the sections state, and every guideline that cannot be checked when an agent acts.\n\nHow to build each policy:\n1. One situation and one outcome per policy. When a clause sets tiers (a lower level that needs someone's approval and a higher level that is not allowed, say), split it into one policy per tier. Each part keeps the clause's reference and its excerpt.\n2. name: a short plain title. category: the kind of rule, in one or two words.\n3. business_area and sub_area: take them from where the policy comes from (the document and the section it sits in, that is, who owns the rule), never from what the policy applies to or mentions. Choose both from the list above and copy them exactly. If no area fits, use the area marked for policies that fit no other area, with sub-area General.\n4. situation: one plain sentence saying when the policy applies, written about the agent and the action it is about to take.\n5. The condition. match is all when every rule must hold, any when one is enough. Each rule has a field, an operator (gt, gte, lt, lte, eq, ne, in, not_in, exists) and a value: value_number for a number, value_text for a single text value, value_list for in and not_in. exists has no value. Use the document's own figure. Wording decides the operator, so copy the document's comparison exactly: above, more than, exceeds, over mean gt (strictly greater; the figure itself does not qualify). At least, or more, not less than mean gte (the figure itself qualifies). Below, less than, under mean lt (strictly smaller). At most, up to, not more than, no more than mean lte (the figure itself qualifies). Never turn above into gte or below into lte.\n6. Use only names that appear in the registry above: the checkpoint, the action tools and the input fields. Never invent a name, and never use a registry name that means something else just because it exists: a field or tool fits only when its plain description is the same thing the document talks about. When the policy needs something the registry has no name for, write it in plain words in unknown_names. When it needs an input that is not received at that checkpoint (an input marked not received yet, or any input at a checkpoint that is not checked yet), list it in missing_inputs with a plain name and the reason.\n7. checkpoint: the point at which the action is checked. action_tools: the registry tools that perform the action. action_plain: those actions in plain words.\n8. outcome: approve when the document says someone must agree before the action happens; block when it says the action is not allowed; notify when it says someone must be told, informed or reported to, and nobody has to agree first. outcome_phrase: the exact words of the document that make it that outcome, copied.\n9. deciders: whoever the document says approves or decides, in the order they decide (for approve). notify: whoever the document says is told, informed or reported to. Use only roles or people the document names, and leave a list empty when it names none. A person who must approve belongs in deciders, never in owner.\n10. reference: the section number exactly as the document shows it. excerpt: the sentence or sentences the policy comes from, copied word for word from the section, with nothing changed, shortened or added.\n11. examples: 3 to 6 example inputs, each value keyed by a registry field, each with the result you expect (approve, block, notify or none). For each number in the condition give a value inside the condition, one outside it and one exactly on the boundary. For each list give a value in it and one not in it. Include an action the policy does not cover.\n12. time_window_from and time_window_to (24-hour HH:MM) and time_zone (an IANA zone name) only when the document states a time window. currency: the three-letter code when the document gives an amount in a currency. amounts_include_tax only when the document says whether amounts include tax.\n13. inputs: every input the condition uses, and anything the person deciding needs to see. For each: a plain name, the registry field, its type, whether it is an amount and its unit, and where it comes from: action (in the action itself), lookup (looked up from a record) or total (a running total over time). show_approver says whether the person deciding sees it; sensitive marks personal or secret data.\n14. The approval details are the deciders and what they see (the inputs with show_approver).\n15. reason_code: a short snake_case code for why the policy applies. message_for_agent: one sentence telling the agent what happened and why. message_for_person: one sentence to the person the agent serves, saying what happens next.\n16. owner: only a person or role the document names as owning, or accountable for, the policy itself. If it names none, owner is null. Never put an approver or someone who is told in owner.\n17. Never mark a policy as confirmed or Active. Every policy you return is a draft for a person to check.\n18. A guideline that cannot be checked at the moment an agent acts (a statement of intent, a duty on people rather than on agents, something that can only be judged afterwards) goes in not_enforceable, with its reference, its excerpt word for word, and the reason in one sentence.\n19. Every value comes from the sections below or from the lists above, never from these instructions. If the sections state no policy, return empty lists.\n\nReply with only JSON matching the schema.\n\nThe sections:\n{sections}"}$p$::jsonb,
 last_modified_by = 'deploy/sql/2026-10-10_agent_policy_prompts_wording.sql'
 WHERE prompt_name = 'agent_policy_extract';

UPDATE proc.bp_prompt SET prompts_desc = $p${"prompt_template": "A person checked a proposed policy for AI agents and found that some of its example results are wrong. Rewrite the policy so that its condition gives the results the person expects. Change only what the corrections need.\n\nThe excerpt the policy comes from, word for word:\n{excerpt}\n\nIts situation now: {situation}\nIts condition now: {condition}\nIts examples now, with the results the agent expected: {examples}\nThe rest of the policy now: {policy}\n\nThe person's corrections. These are constraints the new condition must meet:\n{constraints}\n\nBusiness areas and their sub-areas. These are the only ones that exist:\n{taxonomy}\n\nThe registry: what the orchestrator can see, as field (type, where it comes from):\n{registry}\n\nRules:\n1. Keep the reference and the excerpt exactly as they are. The excerpt stays word for word.\n2. One situation and one outcome. Business area and sub-area come from where the policy comes from, never from what it applies to, and are chosen from the list above.\n3. Use only names that appear in the registry, and only where their plain description is the thing meant. A name the registry lacks goes in unknown_names; an input not received at the checkpoint goes in missing_inputs. Never invent a name.\n4. Give 3 to 6 examples. Include every corrected input with its corrected result, and cover inside, outside and on the boundary for each number and in and out of each list.\n5. Never mark the policy as confirmed or Active.\n6. Wording decides the operator, so copy the document's comparison exactly: above, more than, exceeds, over mean gt (strictly greater; the figure itself does not qualify). At least, or more, not less than mean gte (the figure itself qualifies). Below, less than, under mean lt (strictly smaller). At most, up to, not more than, no more than mean lte (the figure itself qualifies). Never turn above into gte or below into lte. Take the comparison from the excerpt and the corrections.\n7. Every value comes from the excerpt, the policy above, the corrections or the lists above, never from these instructions.\n\nReply with only JSON matching the schema: one policy."}$p$::jsonb,
 last_modified_by = 'deploy/sql/2026-10-10_agent_policy_prompts_wording.sql'
 WHERE prompt_name = 'agent_policy_fix';

